    "anvaya": "अविद्याया आश्रयस्तु शुद्धं ब्रह्मैव ('आश्रयत्वविषयत्वभागिनी निर्विभागचितिरेव केवला'); अज्ञानविरोधि ज्ञानं तु वृत्तिप्रतिबिम्बितं चैतन्यमेव, सूर्यकरस्य सूर्यकान्तावच्छेदेन दाहकत्ववत् ।",
    "bhashyaExtract": "यथा सर्वत्रावभासकः सूर्यालोको मणिमवच्छेद्य तृणदाहकः, तथा स्वयंप्रकाशं चैतन्यं वृत्त्यवच्छेदेनैवाज्ञाननिवर्तकम्; 'मायां तु प्रकृतिं विद्यात्' इति श्रुतिश्च चिदाश्रयत्वे प्रमाणम् ।",
    "englishTranslation": "Pure Consciousness as the Substratum (Āśraya) of Avidyā (Part 1): Addressing the great intra-Advaita and anti-Advaita debate over the locus of primal ignorance, Madhusūdana champions the Vivaraṇa / Saṁkṣepaśārīraka position: Pure undivided Consciousness alone is both the locus (āśraya) and object (viṣaya) of Avidyā. Because the ego (ahaṅkāra) and empirical Jīva are themselves products of Avidyā, they cannot pre-exist to support it—and the sun-crystal analogy resolves the apparent paradox of luminous Consciousness hosting darkness-like Avidyā: just as gentle sunlight peacefully illuminates dry grass until focused through a sun-crystal (sūryakānta) to burn it, Pure Consciousness reveals Avidyā effortlessly and destroys it only when focused through a pramāṇa-vṛtti!\n\nAnswering the objection that 'I am ignorant' shows the ego as locus, he explains this is mutual superimposition (like 'the iron burns' via shared fire-contact), and reconciles texts calling Brahman 'faultless and ever-free' by showing all bondage and agency fall strictly on the reflection (Jīva), never on the prototype (Bimba-Brahman)—quoting Śvetāśvatara 4.10 ('Know Māyā as Prakṛti and the Supreme Lord as Māyin').",
    "commentaryNotes": "Presents the famous Vivaraṇa 'sunlight and sūryakānta lens' analogy explaining how Pure Consciousness hosts Avidyā while vṛtti-reflected Consciousness destroys it.",
